// ============================================================================
// FRAMEWORK-BEHAVIOR · DESTINATIONS  (the cross-process edge)
//
// ── WHY THIS IS ITS OWN PHASE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every phase before this one answers "what does this code mean", and is checkable
// against the language: the Java suite diffs it against javac + javap, and a
// disagreement is a bug. The rules HERE answer a different question — "what does the
// FRAMEWORK do at run time" — and no compiler can adjudicate them. Their oracle is a
// running system: an instrumented JVM, or a trace.
//
// That difference is worth a folder because it is a difference in CONTRACT:
//   - a rule here models a library's behaviour, so it may be version-specific, and
//     every framework NAME it keys on lives in config-resolution/knobs.dl;
//   - a rule here may only ADD relations. It must never change call_chain_edge or any
//     other relation the resolution phases own — a framework model is a weaker kind of
//     knowledge than a resolved call, and folding the two together would silently
//     restate every existing consumer in terms of it;
//   - it is included AFTER call-edge-generation because it CONSUMES the resolved graph.
//
// Other framework-mediated hops belong here as they are built: an @Async or @Scheduled
// handoff, a gRPC stub to its service implementation. Each is a hop no call site
// expresses. An application event published to an @EventListener is NOT one of them:
// it stays in one JVM and is keyed on a type rather than a string, so it is a call edge
// with tier event_dispatch (call-edge-generation/event_dispatch.dl).
//
// A producer and the consumer that serves it live in different deployables. Neither
// calls the other — there is no call site to resolve — so every rule in
// call-edge-generation is silent across that boundary, and a change-impact or
// "who handles this" question stops at the send. What actually ties the two ends
// together is a STRING: a topic, a queue, a routing key, an HTTP route. Both ends of
// that string are already in the IR; nothing joined them.
//
// This file does the join, and emits it as `remote_edge` — deliberately NOT as a
// call_chain_edge. A cross-process hop is not an invocation: it crosses a process,
// it may be asynchronous, and it carries different confidence. Folding it into the
// call graph would silently change the meaning of every existing consumer.
//
//   remote_edge(FromMethod, ToMethod, Transport, Destination, Confidence)
//     Transport   kafka | amqp | jms | sqs | stream | http
//     Confidence  exact      the two ends spell the destination identically
//                 route_shape an HTTP route matched on its literal prefix and its
//                            segment count, which is how a client that concatenates
//                            a path meets a server that declares a template
//
// The name lists this joins on are in knobs.dl, section (8) — adding a broker or an
// HTTP client is a fact there, not a rule here.
// ============================================================================

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. RESOLVING A DESTINATION STRING
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A destination is written three ways and all three have to end at the same value,
// or the two ends of one conversation will not meet:
//   "orders.created"                  a literal
//   "${app.topics.orders-created}"    a configured key, resolved through the YAML
//   Topics.ORDERS_CREATED             a constant declared elsewhere

// dest_resolved(Raw, Value) — what a raw annotation/argument string denotes.
// (a) no placeholder: the string is the destination.
dest_resolved(v, v) :- dest_raw(v), !contains("${", v).
// (b) a placeholder that a configuration file defines.
dest_resolved(v, d) :- dest_raw(v), cfg_placeholder(v, k, _), config_key_value(_, k, d).
// (c) a placeholder with an inline default and no file to override it. The default is
//     what the framework would use, so it is the honest answer rather than nothing.
dest_resolved(v, d) :- dest_raw(v), cfg_placeholder(v, k, d), d != "",
    !config_key_value(_, k, _).

// ── constants ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The parser leaves referencedEntityHash empty for a field reference, so a constant
// is matched by NAME — which is only sound if the name is unambiguous. A name that
// several client constants declare with DIFFERENT values resolves to nothing rather
// than to an arbitrary one.
// const_string(Name, Value): a static final String whose initializer is a literal.
// An interface field is implicitly static final; the parser stamps both modifiers on
// it, so the modifier test below covers `interface Keys { String PREFIX = "x"; }` too.
const_string_owned(n, o, q, v) :- java_field(n, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, o, q, _, mods, f),
    contains("STATIC", mods), contains("FINAL", mods),
    expr_owner("client", f, _, e), expr_literal("client", v, e).
const_string(n, v) :- const_string_owned(n, _, _, v).
const_string_values(n, c) :- const_string(n, _), c = count : { const_string(n, _) }.
const_string_unique(n, v) :- const_string(n, v), const_string_values(n, 1).

// const_string_ref(Ref, Value): a constant as an argument WRITES it -- bare (`PREFIX`,
// a static import or the declaring type itself), or qualified by any trailing part of
// its owner's name (`Keys.PREFIX`, `Constants.Auth.PREFIX`, `com.acme.Keys.PREFIX`).
// A spelling resolves only when every constant it can denote has one value: two
// `Keys` types with different PREFIX values leave `Keys.PREFIX` unresolved.
// Demand-driven (const_ref_demand, filled by the rules that read a reference), and
// joined on the reference's last segment, so no rule pairs every reference with every
// constant.
const_ref_dot(r, i) :- const_ref_demand(r), i = range(0, strlen(r)), substr(r, i, 1) = ".".
const_ref_last(r, r) :- const_ref_demand(r), !const_ref_dot(r, _).
const_ref_last(r, substr(r, i + 1, strlen(r) - i - 1)) :- const_ref_demand(r),
    const_ref_dot(r, _), i = max j : { const_ref_dot(r, j) }.
const_string_ref_cand(r, v) :- const_ref_last(r, r), const_string_owned(r, _, _, v).
const_string_ref_cand(r, v) :- const_ref_last(r, n), r != n, const_string_owned(n, _, q, v),
    full = cat(q, cat(".", n)), strlen(full) >= strlen(r), strlen(full) - strlen(r) = d,
    substr(full, d, strlen(r)) = r, ( d = 0 ; d > 0, substr(full, d - 1, 1) = "." ).
const_string_ref(r, v) :- const_string_ref_cand(r, v), 1 = count : { const_string_ref_cand(r, _) }.

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. WHAT A METHOD SERVES
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// (a) a listener annotation naming its topic/queue.
dest_raw(v) :- ann_on_method(_, a, n, _, _), cfg_serves_ann(n, _, arg), ann_arg(_, a, arg, v, _, _).
serves_destination(m, tr, d) :- ann_on_method(_, a, n, m, _), cfg_serves_ann(n, tr, arg),
    ann_arg(_, a, arg, v, _, _), dest_resolved(v, d).
// … and the same annotation written with a constant instead of a literal.
serves_destination(m, tr, d) :- ann_on_method(_, a, n, m, _), cfg_serves_ann(n, tr, arg),
    ann_arg(_, a, arg, v, _, _), const_string_unique(v, d).

// (b) an HTTP route. The path is the type's prefix followed by the method's remainder,
//     and either half may be absent.
dest_raw(v) :- ann_on_type(_, a, n, _), cfg_route_ann(n), cfg_route_arg(arg), ann_arg(_, a, arg, v, _, _).
dest_raw(v) :- ann_on_method(_, a, n, _, _), cfg_route_ann(n), cfg_route_arg(arg), ann_arg(_, a, arg, v, _, _).

route_base(t, b) :- ann_on_type(_, a, n, t), cfg_route_ann(n), cfg_route_arg(arg),
    ann_arg(_, a, arg, v, _, _), dest_resolved(v, b).
// A type with a route-annotated method but no route annotation of its own contributes
// no prefix; without this clause such a method would have no route at all.
route_base(t, "") :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, _, t), cfg_route_ann(n), !route_base_declared(t).
// … and the same when the methods carry only a verb annotation, so a resource class
// with no @Path of its own still gives its handlers a (empty) base to inherit.
route_base(t, "") :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, _, t), cfg_route_verb_ann(n, _), !route_base_declared(t).
route_base_declared(t) :- ann_on_type(_, a, n, t), cfg_route_ann(n), cfg_route_arg(arg),
    ann_arg(_, a, arg, _, _, _).

// the method's own half — present, or absent (a bare @PostMapping on the class route).
route_tail(m, s) :- ann_on_method(_, a, n, m, _), cfg_route_ann(n), cfg_route_arg(arg),
    ann_arg(_, a, arg, v, _, _), dest_resolved(v, s).
route_tail(m, "") :- ann_on_method(_, a, n, m, _), cfg_route_ann(n), !route_tail_declared(a).
route_tail_declared(a) :- ann_on_method(_, a, n, _, _), cfg_route_ann(n), cfg_route_arg(arg),
    ann_arg(_, a, arg, _, _, _).

// Which END a route annotation describes is decided by the TYPE first. A Feign
// interface declares its methods with @GetMapping — the same annotation a controller
// uses — so keying off the annotation alone makes a client look like a handler, and it
// then answers "who serves this route" with somebody else's outbound call.
route_type_is_client(t) :- ann_on_type(_, _, n, t), cfg_client_type_ann(n).

route_raw_own(m, path, "server") :- route_tail(m, tail), ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, owner),
    cfg_route_ann(n), cfg_route_server_ann(n), !route_type_is_client(owner),
    route_base(owner, base), path_join(base, tail, path).
route_raw_own(m, path, "client") :- route_tail(m, tail), ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, owner),
    cfg_route_ann(n), !cfg_route_server_ann(n), route_base(owner, base), path_join(base, tail, path).
route_raw_own(m, path, "client") :- route_tail(m, tail), ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, owner),
    cfg_route_ann(n), route_type_is_client(owner), route_base(owner, base), path_join(base, tail, path).
// A handler that names only its VERB still has a path: its resource class's. The
// verb annotation contributes no remainder of its own, so the route is the base and
// nothing more, which is what JAX-RS means by @GET on a method under @Path("/orders").
// Guarded by !method_route_ann: a method carrying BOTH @GET and @Path("/{id}") takes
// its remainder from the @Path, and without the guard it would ALSO produce a second,
// shorter route at the bare class path.
method_route_ann(m) :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, _), cfg_route_ann(n).
route_raw_own(m, base, "server") :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, owner),
    cfg_route_verb_ann(n, _), !method_route_ann(m),
    !route_type_is_client(owner), route_base(owner, base).
// … and the same shape on a declarative client interface (MicroProfile @RegisterRestClient).
route_raw_own(m, base, "client") :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, owner),
    cfg_route_verb_ann(n, _), !method_route_ann(m),
    route_type_is_client(owner), route_base(owner, base).
route_raw_of_method(m, p, side) :- route_raw_own(m, p, side), method_owner("client", owner, m),
    !locator_only_resource(owner).

// ── joining the two halves: exactly one '/' between them ────────────────────
// JAX-RS and Spring both insert the separator when they concatenate a class path and a
// method path, and drop a doubled one: @Path("/items") + @Path("{id}") is /items/{id},
// and @RequestMapping("/api/") + @GetMapping("/x") is /api/x. A bare cat() gave
// /items{id} and /api//x, neither of which any client writes.
path_join_demand(base, tail) :- route_tail(m, tail), ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, owner),
    cfg_route_ann(n), route_base(owner, base).
path_join(b, t, t) :- path_join_demand(b, t), b = "".
path_join(b, t, b) :- path_join_demand(b, t), b != "", t = "".
path_join(b, t, j) :- path_join_demand(b, t), path_ends_slash(b), path_starts_slash(t),
    j = cat(b, substr(t, 1, strlen(t) - 1)).
path_join(b, t, j) :- path_join_demand(b, t), path_ends_slash(b), path_starts_other(t),
    j = cat(b, t).
path_join(b, t, j) :- path_join_demand(b, t), path_ends_other(b), path_starts_slash(t),
    j = cat(b, t).
path_join(b, t, j) :- path_join_demand(b, t), path_ends_other(b), path_starts_other(t),
    j = cat(b, cat("/", t)).
path_ends_slash(b) :- path_join_demand(b, _), strlen(b) > 0, substr(b, strlen(b) - 1, 1) = "/".
path_starts_slash(t) :- path_join_demand(_, t), strlen(t) > 0, substr(t, 0, 1) = "/".
// the negative halves are stated positively: the locator rules feed path_join_demand from
// a route that path_join itself produced, so a negation here would be a cycle through it
path_ends_other(b) :- path_join_demand(b, _), strlen(b) > 0, substr(b, strlen(b) - 1, 1) != "/".
path_starts_other(t) :- path_join_demand(_, t), strlen(t) > 0, substr(t, 0, 1) != "/".

// ── JAX-RS SUB-RESOURCE LOCATORS ────────────────────────────────────────────
// A method with @Path and no verb returns the object that serves the rest of the request:
//     @Path("/widgets") class Widgets { @Path("/{id}") Widget find(..) { .. } }
//     class Widget { @GET String details() { .. } }
// Widget.details serves GET /widgets/{id}. The locator's declared return type is the
// resource; its verb methods take the locator's route, and its own @Path methods extend
// it. One level only: a resource that returns its own type would otherwise grow the
// route without end.
jaxrs_locator(m, r) :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, owner), cfg_subresource_locator_ann(n),
    !method_verb_declared(m), !route_type_is_client(owner),
    method_return_type_resolves("client", m, _, r), r != owner.
locator_base(r, raw) :- jaxrs_locator(m, r), route_raw_own(m, raw, "server").
// A class with no @Path of its own that a locator returns is not a root resource: its
// methods are served only below the locator, never at their own bare path.
locator_only_resource(r) :- jaxrs_locator(_, r), !route_base_declared(r).
route_raw_of_method(m, raw, "server") :- locator_base(r, raw), ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, r),
    cfg_route_verb_ann(n, _), !method_route_ann(m).
route_raw_of_method(m, p, "server") :- locator_base(r, raw), route_tail(m, tail),
    ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, r), cfg_route_ann(n), cfg_route_server_ann(n), path_join(raw, tail, p).
path_join_demand(raw, tail) :- locator_base(r, raw), route_tail(m, tail), ann_on_method(_, _, _, m, r).

// ── SERVLETS MAPPED TO A URL PATTERN ────────────────────────────────────────
// web.xml maps a servlet to its patterns through the <servlet-name> both elements carry:
//   <servlet><servlet-name>w</servlet-name><servlet-class>app.W</servlet-class></servlet>
//   <servlet-mapping><servlet-name>w</servlet-name><url-pattern>/widgets</url-pattern></servlet-mapping>
// and @WebServlet("/widgets") / urlPatterns = {..} says the same on the class. The servlet's
// do* methods serve the pattern, each for its own verb.
servlet_decl_name(t, name, fp) :- xml_container_class("web_servlet", t, elem),
    xml_elem("client", _, _, _, parent, fp, elem),
    xml_elem("client", nt, _, raw, parent, _, _), cfg_xml_servlet_name_elem(nt),
    cfg_clean(raw, name), name != "".
servlet_mapped_pattern(name, pat, fp) :- xml_elem("client", mt, _, _, _, fp, mp), cfg_xml_servlet_mapping_elem(mt),
    xml_elem("client", nt, _, rawn, mp, _, _), cfg_xml_servlet_name_elem(nt), cfg_clean(rawn, name),
    xml_elem("client", pt, _, rawp, mp, _, _), cfg_xml_url_pattern_elem(pt), cfg_clean(rawp, pat).
servlet_pattern(t, pat) :- servlet_decl_name(t, name, fp), servlet_mapped_pattern(name, pat, fp).
servlet_pattern(t, pat) :- ann_on_type("client", a, "WebServlet", t), cfg_servlet_url_arg(arg),
    ann_arg("client", a, arg, pat, "STRING_LITERAL", _).
// An exact pattern is the route. A path-prefix pattern "/widgets/*" serves every path
// below /widgets, which the join can only express as a template: /widgets/{path} meets a
// client that concatenates "/widgets/" + id by route shape, never exactly. The catch-all
// "/*", the default servlet "/" and an extension pattern "*.do" name no route.
servlet_route_raw(t, pat) :- servlet_pattern(t, pat), !contains("*", pat), pat != "/", pat != "".
servlet_route_raw(t, r) :- servlet_pattern(t, pat), strlen(pat) > 2,
    substr(pat, strlen(pat) - 2, 2) = "/*", pre = substr(pat, 0, strlen(pat) - 2),
    !contains("*", pre), r = cat(pre, "/{path}").
servlet_handler(m, t, v) :- servlet_route_raw(t, _),
    java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "INSTANCE_METHOD", _, _, _, _, m),
    cfg_servlet_verb_method(name, v).
route_declared(raw) :- servlet_route_raw(_, raw).
serves_destination(m, "http", u) :- servlet_handler(m, t, _), servlet_route_raw(t, raw), url_path(raw, u).

route_declared(raw) :- route_raw_of_method(_, raw, _).
// A declared route goes through the same normalisation as a client's URL, so
// @GetMapping("pets/visits") and a client's "…/pets/visits?petId={id}" meet.
route_of_method(m, norm, side) :- route_raw_of_method(m, raw, side), url_path(raw, norm).

serves_destination(m, "http", p) :- route_of_method(m, p, "server").

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. WHAT A METHOD SENDS TO
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A method NAME alone is not enough to say a call is a send. `send` is one of the
// commonest method names there is, and matching it bare read this project's own
// `dispatcher.send("email", …)` as a Kafka produce to a topic called "email". So the
// RECEIVER's declared type has to be a producer before any argument is read.
recv_declared_simple(recv, t) :- recv_is_field(recv, f),
    java_field(_, _, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, f).
recv_declared_simple(recv, t) :- recv_is_local(recv, l),
    java_local_variable(_, _, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, l).
// … and a chain that starts on a static factory rather than an injected field:
// WebClient.create(base).get().uri(path). The chain root is then the TYPE NAME itself,
// which is only accepted because it has to be a name knobs.dl already calls a producer.
recv_declared_simple(recv, t) :- expr_reference_name(recv, t), cfg_sends_owner(t, _).

// Real client code is a fluent chain — webClientBuilder.build().get().uri(path) —
// so the receiver of the call that carries the path is the RESULT of the previous
// call, not the field. Walking the chain back to what it started on is what makes
// the common shape visible at all; requiring the immediate receiver to be the field
// found nothing on a real multi-service codebase.
recv_root(r, r) :- call_site(_, _, r).
recv_root(r, root) :- call_site(_, _, r), call_site(r, _, inner), recv_root(inner, root).

sends_via(call, tr) :- call_site(call, _, recv), recv_root(recv, root),
    recv_declared_simple(root, t), cfg_sends_owner(t, tr).

// (a) a declarative HTTP client: the interface method IS the send.
sends_destination(m, "http", p) :- route_of_method(m, p, "client").

// (b) the argument that names the destination, in each of the ways one is written.
//     call_arg(Call, Index, Expr) — the argument expressions of a call site.
call_arg(call, idx, arg) :- call_site(call, _, _), expr_child("client", call, "ARGUMENT", arg),
    expr_position("client", arg, idx).

// arg_destination(Expr, Value) — what an argument expression denotes as a destination.
//   a literal, guarded on the expression KIND: literalValue also holds the NAME of a
//   reference, so without this guard a field called `cancelledTopic` would be read as
//   a topic called "cancelledTopic".
arg_destination(arg, d) :- expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, arg),
    expr_literal("client", v, arg), dest_resolved(v, d).
//   a reference to a constant declared elsewhere.
arg_destination(arg, d) :- expr_reference_name(arg, n), const_string_unique(n, d).
//   a reference to a field whose value comes from configuration.
arg_destination(arg, d) :- expr_reference_name(arg, n), field_named_config_value(n, d).
//   a getter on a @ConfigurationProperties bean: properties.getItemPath().
arg_destination(arg, d) :- call_site(arg, gn, _), props_getter_value(gn, d).

// the NAME a reference expression carries (the parser stores it in literalValue and
// leaves referencedEntityHash empty, so a reference is matched by name).
expr_reference_name(e, n) :- expr_kind("client", "FIELD_ACCESS", _, e),
    expr_literal("client", n, e).
expr_reference_name(e, n) :- expr_kind("client", "IDENTIFIER_REFERENCE", _, e),
    expr_literal("client", n, e).

// A field whose value is a configuration value — bound directly, or, far more often,
// injected through a constructor parameter of the same name carrying @Value.
field_config_key(f, k) :- config_binding(k, _, "field", f, _).
field_config_key(f, k) :- java_field(n, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, owner, _, _, _, _, f),
    java_method_parameter(n, _, ctor, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, prm),
    method_owner("client", owner, ctor), config_binding(k, _, "param", prm, ctor).
field_named_config_value(n, d) :- java_field(n, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, f),
    field_config_key(f, k), config_key_value(_, k, d).

// A getter on a @ConfigurationProperties type, mapped to the value its backing
// field is bound to.
cfg_getter_demand(p) :- config_binding(_, "config_properties", "field", f, _),
    java_field(p, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, f).
cfg_getter_name(p, g) :- cfg_getter_demand(p), strlen(p) > 0,
    cfg_upper(substr(p, 0, 1), up), g = cat("get", cat(up, substr(p, 1, strlen(p) - 1))).
props_getter_value(g, d) :- config_binding(k, "config_properties", "field", f, _),
    java_field(fn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, f),
    cfg_getter_name(fn, g), config_key_value(_, k, d).

// (c) a broker send, whose destination is a positional argument.
dest_raw(v) :- sends_via(call, _), call_site(call, name, _), cfg_sends_method(name, _, idx),
    call_arg(call, idx, arg), expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, arg),
    expr_literal("client", v, arg).
// Carries the CALL, like the HTTP clause, because remote_unserved is judged per call —
// without it a broker send whose destination nothing here consumes vanished from the
// report entirely instead of being listed.
sends_destination_at(call, m, tr, d) :- sends_via(call, tr), call_site(call, name, _),
    cfg_sends_method(name, tr, idx), call_arg(call, idx, arg), arg_destination(arg, d),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", call, m).

// (d) an imperative HTTP call. The URL is routinely built by concatenation, so the
//     destination is any path-shaped string written anywhere inside the URL argument.
// Only the URL argument. A RestTemplate send takes (url, request body, ..): reading every
// argument made a body literal ("shipped") a destination of its own, which linked the
// call to whatever handler served /shipped and, as a second fragment, cost it the route
// it really calls. Every method in cfg_sends_http takes the URL first.
http_url_arg(c, a) :- sends_via(c, "http"), call_site(c, name, _), cfg_sends_http(name),
    call_arg(c, "0", a).
expr_descendant(a, a) :- http_url_arg(_, a).
expr_descendant(a, d) :- expr_descendant(a, c), expr_child("client", c, _, d).
// Real code builds the URL into a local and passes the local:
//     String url = base + "/api/x/" + id + "/detail";
//     http.getForObject(url, …)
// so the argument subtree is a single identifier and every fragment is in the local's
// initializer, which hangs off the LOCAL as its owner rather than off the call.
expr_descendant(a, d) :- expr_descendant(a, c), expr_reference_name(c, n),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", c, m),
    java_local_variable(n, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, m, _, _, _, _, lv),
    expr_owner("client", lv, _, d).

http_arg_literal(v) :- http_url_arg(_, arg), expr_descendant(arg, d), expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, d),
    expr_literal("client", v, d).
// A URL literal is a destination in its own right. Without this the only client paths
// that resolved were the ones that happened to be spelled identically to a declared
// route somewhere else in the tree — which looked like it worked, on exactly one case.
dest_raw(v) :- http_arg_literal(v).
path_candidate(v) :- http_arg_literal(v).
path_candidate(v) :- const_string(_, v).
path_candidate(v) :- config_key_value(_, _, v).
// A path is recognised by its leading slash. The candidate set is kept separate from
// dest_raw on purpose: deriving the shape from dest_raw, while dest_raw is derived
// from the shape, is a cycle that admits nothing.
// Path-shaped means: it survives normalisation and names more than a bare word.
// Only the prefix rule consults this; an exact match needs no shape filter, because
// equality with a declared route is a far stronger test than any shape test.
http_path_shaped(u) :- url_path(_, u), strlen(u) >= 2, contains("/", substr(u, 1, strlen(u) - 1)).
// NOTE — deliberately NOT `dest_raw(u) :- url_path(_, u)`. Placeholder resolution
// happens BEFORE normalisation, so feeding normalised paths back into the raw set
// puts url_path and dest_raw in one cycle with negation in it, which Soufflé cannot
// stratify. A normalised path needs no second resolution pass.

// ── URL NORMALISATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Both ends of one route are written differently in every real codebase:
//   "http://customers-service/owners/{ownerId}"   a client, with scheme and host
//   "pets/visits?petId={petId}"                   a client, with a query string
//   "vets"                                        a client, with no leading slash
//   @RequestMapping("/vets")                      the server, with one
// Comparing them raw matches none of these, so every route is reduced to a path:
// scheme and authority removed, query string removed, leading slash ensured.
url_raw(v) :- path_candidate(v).
url_raw(v) :- route_declared(v).

url_scheme_at(s, i) :- url_raw(s), strlen(s) >= 3,
    i = range(0, strlen(s) - 2), substr(s, i, 3) = "://".
url_after_scheme(s, r) :- url_scheme_at(s, i), r = substr(s, i + 3, strlen(s) - i - 3).
url_slash_in(r, i) :- url_after_scheme(_, r), i = range(0, strlen(r)), substr(r, i, 1) = "/".
url_no_scheme(s, s) :- url_raw(s), !contains("://", s).
url_no_scheme(s, p) :- url_after_scheme(s, r), m = min i : url_slash_in(r, i),
    p = substr(r, m, strlen(r) - m).
url_no_scheme(s, "/") :- url_after_scheme(s, r), !url_slash_in(r, _).

url_q(p, i) :- url_no_scheme(_, p), i = range(0, strlen(p)), substr(p, i, 1) = "?".
url_no_query(p, p) :- url_no_scheme(_, p), !url_q(p, _).
url_no_query(p, q) :- url_no_scheme(_, p), m = min i : url_q(p, i), q = substr(p, 0, m).

url_path(s, u) :- url_no_scheme(s, p), url_no_query(p, q), strlen(q) > 0,
    substr(q, 0, 1) = "/", u = q.
url_path(s, u) :- url_no_scheme(s, p), url_no_query(p, q), strlen(q) > 0,
    substr(q, 0, 1) != "/", u = cat("/", q).

sends_destination_at(call, m, "http", u) :- http_url_arg(call, arg), expr_descendant(arg, d),
    arg_destination(d, p), url_path(p, u), expr_ultimate_method("client", call, m).
sends_destination(m, tr, u) :- sends_destination_at(_, m, tr, u).

// (e) the JAX-RS client: client.target("http://shop/orders").path("{id}").request().get(..).
//     The URL is built across the chain, so it is followed from target(..) through each
//     path(..) to request(..) and the verb that sends it. Kept apart from sends_via: the
//     verb methods (put, delete) share names with RestTemplate's URL-first sends, and
//     reading put(Entity.json(x))'s argument as a URL is the defect (d) above avoids.
// The chain root is declared as the JAX-RS Client / WebTarget, and that name does not resolve
// to a class of the project (a project's own `Client` with a target(..) method is not the
// API), or is typed as the library's; or the chain starts on ClientBuilder itself.
jaxrs_client_root(root) :- recv_type_simple(root, n), cfg_jaxrs_client_owner(n).
jaxrs_client_root(root) :- recv_declared_simple(root, n), cfg_jaxrs_client_owner(n),
    !expr_type("client", root, _).
jaxrs_client_root(root) :- expr_reference_name(root, n), cfg_jaxrs_client_factory(n),
    !simple_name_declared(n).
jaxrs_target(call, d) :- call_site(call, "target", recv), recv_root(recv, root),
    jaxrs_client_root(root), call_arg(call, "0", arg), arg_destination(arg, d).
jaxrs_target(call, j) :- call_site(call, "path", recv), jaxrs_target(recv, base),
    call_arg(call, "0", arg), expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, arg),
    expr_literal("client", v, arg), path_join(base, v, j).
path_join_demand(base, v) :- call_site(call, "path", recv), jaxrs_target(recv, base),
    call_arg(call, "0", arg), expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, arg), expr_literal("client", v, arg).
dest_raw(v) :- call_site(call, "target", _), call_arg(call, "0", arg),
    expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, arg), expr_literal("client", v, arg).
jaxrs_builder(call, d) :- call_site(call, "request", tgt), jaxrs_target(tgt, d).
jaxrs_builder(call, d) :- call_site(call, n, inner), cfg_jaxrs_builder_step(n), jaxrs_builder(inner, d).
jaxrs_send(call, m, d, v) :- call_site(call, n, b), cfg_jaxrs_invoke_verb(n, v), jaxrs_builder(b, d),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", call, m).
path_candidate(d) :- jaxrs_send(_, _, d, _).
sends_destination_at(call, m, "http", u) :- jaxrs_send(call, m, d, _), url_path(d, u).

// EVERY path fragment this one call writes. A URL built by concatenation leaves more
// than one — "/api/inventory/" + sku + "/reserved" leaves two — and a prefix rule that
// looks at only the first will answer the route that the second one rules out.
send_fragment(call, f) :- http_url_arg(call, arg),
    expr_descendant(arg, d), expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, d),
    expr_literal("client", raw, d), url_path(raw, f).
// NOTE: deliberately NOT gated on http_path_shaped. That guard wants two segments,
// and the fragment that rules a route out is usually a single one — "/reserved".
// Gating this on it let /api/inventory/{sku}/reserved be reported as
// /api/inventory/{sku}, which is the exact wrong edge the guard exists to stop.
// A candidate route that does not contain one of this call's own fragments is not the
// route this call names. Measured: without it, a client reaching
// /api/inventory/{sku}/reserved was reported as reaching /api/inventory/{sku}.
route_contradicted(call, s) :- send_fragment(call, f), serves_destination(_, "http", s),
    !contains(f, s).

// ── RECONSTRUCTING A CONCATENATED ROUTE ─────────────────────────────────────
// A concatenation leaves several fragments and no single one is the route. Ordered by
// where they are written, the FIRST is the route's literal prefix and the LAST is its
// tail, and the segment counts must add up — which is what keeps
// /api/x/{id}/reserve away from /api/x/{id}/reservation.
send_frag_at(call, f, key) :- http_url_arg(call, arg),
    expr_descendant(arg, d), expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, d),
    expr_literal("client", raw, d), url_path(raw, f),
    expr_src_pos("client", d, line, col),
    key = to_number(line) * 10000 + to_number(col).
send_frag_key_min(call, k) :- send_frag_at(call, _, _), k = min j : send_frag_at(call, _, j).
send_frag_key_max(call, k) :- send_frag_at(call, _, _), k = max j : send_frag_at(call, _, j).
send_frag_first(call, f) :- send_frag_key_min(call, k), send_frag_at(call, f, k).
send_frag_last(call, f)  :- send_frag_key_max(call, k), send_frag_at(call, f, k).
send_frag_count(call, n) :- send_frag_at(call, _, _), n = count : { send_frag_at(call, _, _) }.
send_frag_segsum(call, n) :- send_frag_at(call, _, _),
    n = sum c : { send_frag_at(call, f, _), route_segments_of(f, c) }.
// segment counts for a fragment, which is not necessarily a declared route
route_segments_of(f, n) :- send_frag_at(_, f, _),
    n = count : { i = range(0, strlen(f)), substr(f, i, 1) = "/" }.

// The offset is CLAMPED rather than merely guarded. Soufflé inlines a simple helper
// back into its caller, so splitting the length test into its own relation did not stop
// `substr(s, strlen(s) - strlen(f), …)` being evaluated on the pairs where f is longer
// than s — 657 warnings on one real codebase. Clamping makes the functor total; the
// guard still decides the answer.
route_suffix_ok(s, f, off) :- serves_destination(_, "http", s), send_frag_at(_, f, _),
    strlen(s) >= strlen(f), off = max(0, strlen(s) - strlen(f)).
route_ends_with(s, f) :- route_suffix_ok(s, f, off),
    substr(s, off, min(strlen(f), strlen(s))) = f.

remote_edge_via(call, from, to, "http", s, "route_built") :- sends_destination_at(call, from, "http", _),
    serves_destination(to, "http", s), from != to,
    send_frag_count(call, n), n > 1,
    send_frag_first(call, first), route_prefix(s, first),
    send_frag_last(call, last), first != last, route_ends_with(s, last),
    send_frag_segsum(call, segs), route_segments(s, segs),
    !route_contradicted(call, s),
    send_verb(from, sv), method_verb(to, mv), verb_compatible(sv, mv).

// ── AMQP: THE BINDING IS THE HOP ────────────────────────────────────────────
// A publisher names an EXCHANGE and a ROUTING KEY; a listener names a QUEUE. They are
// different strings and match only through the binding that ties them together — which
// is itself a @Bean, built from other @Beans:
//     @Bean Queue q()          { return new Queue("inventory.q"); }
//     @Bean TopicExchange e()  { return new TopicExchange("inventory.exchange"); }
//     @Bean Binding b(Queue q, TopicExchange e) {
//         return BindingBuilder.bind(q).to(e).with("inventory.reserve"); }
// Spring resolves those parameters to the beans of the same NAME, which is what makes
// the chain followable without modelling the container.

// the fluent binding chain, read from its last call backwards
amqp_bind_rk(w, rk) :- call_site(w, "with", _), call_arg(w, "0", a),
    expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, a), expr_literal("client", rk, a).
amqp_bind_exchange(w, e) :- call_site(w, "with", to), call_site(to, "to", _),
    call_arg(to, "0", a), expr_reference_name(a, e).
amqp_bind_queue(w, q) :- call_site(w, "with", to), call_site(to, "to", bind),
    call_site(bind, "bind", _), call_arg(bind, "0", a), expr_reference_name(a, q).
amqp_bind_chain(w, q, e, rk) :- amqp_bind_rk(w, rk), amqp_bind_exchange(w, e),
    amqp_bind_queue(w, q).

// a @Bean whose body constructs something from one string literal: that literal is the
// AMQP name the bean stands for. Demanded only for beans a binding actually names, so
// this never walks every constructor in the tree.
amqp_bean_demand(n) :- amqp_bind_chain(_, n, _, _).
amqp_bean_demand(n) :- amqp_bind_chain(_, _, n, _).
amqp_bean_name(mname, name) :- amqp_bean_demand(mname),
    java_method(mname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, m),
    ann_on_method(_, _, "Bean", m, _),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", e, m), expr_kind("client", "OBJECT_CREATION", _, e),
    expr_child("client", e, "ARGUMENT", a), expr_position("client", a, "0"),
    expr_kind("client", "LITERAL", _, a), expr_literal("client", name, a).

amqp_binding(exchange, rk, queue) :- amqp_bind_chain(_, qref, eref, rk),
    amqp_bean_name(qref, queue), amqp_bean_name(eref, exchange).

// what a publisher names. convertAndSend is overloaded: (routingKey, payload) names no
// exchange, (exchange, routingKey, payload) does, so the arity decides which argument
// is which. Reading argument 1 unconditionally would call the PAYLOAD a routing key.
call_arg_count(call, n) :- call_site(call, _, _), n = count : { call_arg(call, _, _) }.
amqp_publish(call, m, exchange, rk) :- sends_via(call, "amqp"), call_site(call, name, _),
    cfg_sends_method(name, "amqp", _), call_arg_count(call, n), n >= 3,
    call_arg(call, "0", ae), arg_destination(ae, exchange),
    call_arg(call, "1", re), arg_destination(re, rk),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", call, m).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. THE JOIN
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A route is a path AND a verb. Matching on the path alone made one client call an
// edge to every handler on that path — a GET to /owners linked to the @PostMapping
// that creates one, which is a wrong answer to "what does this call reach".
// An end whose verb cannot be determined stays compatible with all of them, so this
// filters wrong edges without dropping ones it merely cannot judge.
verb_compatible(a, b) :- http_verb_known(a), http_verb_known(b), a = b.
verb_compatible(a, "any") :- http_verb_known(a).
verb_compatible("any", b) :- http_verb_known(b).
http_verb_known("any"). http_verb_known("GET"). http_verb_known("POST").
http_verb_known("PUT"). http_verb_known("DELETE"). http_verb_known("PATCH").
http_verb_known("HEAD"). http_verb_known("OPTIONS").

// the server's verb comes from which mapping annotation it used …
method_verb(m, v) :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, _), cfg_route_ann(n), cfg_route_verb(n, v).
// A sibling verb annotation names the verb, so a method carrying @Path AND @GET must
// NOT also fall back to "any": "any" is compatible with every client verb, so leaving
// it in place would keep the collision this narrows. The verb stays a FILTER on the
// join rather than part of the path, so a client whose verb cannot be determined still
// matches the path.
method_verb(m, v) :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, _), cfg_route_verb_ann(n, v).
method_verb_declared(m) :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, _), cfg_route_verb_ann(n, _).
method_verb(m, "any") :- ann_on_method(_, _, n, m, _), cfg_route_ann(n), !cfg_route_verb_known(n), !method_verb_declared(m).
cfg_route_verb_known(n) :- cfg_route_verb(n, _).
// a servlet's doGet serves GET, its service(..) every verb
method_verb(m, v) :- servlet_handler(m, _, v).
// … the client's from the call it made, or from its own exchange annotation.
call_verb(call, v) :- call_site(call, name, _), cfg_sends_verb(name, v).
// a fluent chain names the verb one call earlier: .get().uri(path)
call_verb(call, v) :- call_site(call, _, recv), call_site(recv, name, _), cfg_sends_verb(name, v).

send_verb(m, v) :- sends_via(call, "http"), expr_ultimate_method("client", call, m), call_verb(call, v).
send_verb(m, "any") :- sends_destination(m, "http", _), !send_verb_known(m).
send_verb_known(m) :- sends_via(call, "http"), expr_ultimate_method("client", call, m), call_verb(call, _).
send_verb(m, v) :- jaxrs_send(_, m, _, v).
send_verb_known(m) :- jaxrs_send(_, m, _, _).
send_verb(m, v) :- route_of_method(m, _, "client"), method_verb(m, v).

// ── gRPC: the generated stub and the handler it serves (#1108) ──────────────────────────────
// Unlike a topic or a route, the join key here is not a string a developer wrote: protoc emits
// <S>ImplBase (server) and <S>BlockingStub / <S>Stub / <S>FutureStub (client) as NESTED types of the
// same <S>Grpc holder. So the holder's qualified name plus the method name pairs the two ends without
// reading the .proto, which is never in the source tree anyway.
//
// The server end already exists: entry-points.dl marks an ImplBase override as a grpc_service entry
// point. What was missing is the other half -- the client call -- so `impact` on a handler reported no
// callers and `path` said the two were independent, which is the honest answer to the wrong question.
//
// Keyed on the HOLDER rather than the service's simple name: two protos may both declare Get, and the
// holder is what distinguishes them. Arity is not part of the key: a blocking stub takes (request) and
// the async stub takes (request, observer) for the SAME rpc, so requiring equal arity would drop the
// async shape, which is the one a streaming service uses.
grpc_holder(t, holder) :- java_type(name, qname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t),
    cfg_stub_client_suffix(suffix),
    strlen(name) >= strlen(suffix),
    substr(name, max(0, strlen(name) - strlen(suffix)), min(strlen(suffix), strlen(name))) = suffix,
    strlen(qname) > strlen(name),
    holder = substr(qname, 0, max(0, strlen(qname) - strlen(name) - 1)).

// the handler's holder, from the ImplBase it extends -- same derivation, the server suffix
grpc_impl_holder(t, holder) :- type_ancestor(t, base),
    java_type(bname, bqname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, base),
    cfg_stub_base_suffix(suffix, "grpc_service"),
    strlen(bname) >= strlen(suffix),
    substr(bname, max(0, strlen(bname) - strlen(suffix)), min(strlen(suffix), strlen(bname))) = suffix,
    strlen(bqname) > strlen(bname),
    holder = substr(bqname, 0, max(0, strlen(bqname) - strlen(bname) - 1)).

// a handler method: declared on a type whose base is an ImplBase under that holder
grpc_serves(m, key) :- grpc_impl_holder(t, holder), method_owner("client", t, m),
    java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "INSTANCE_METHOD", _, _, _, _, m),
    name != "", key = cat(holder, cat("/", name)).

// a client call: a call site whose receiver is a generated stub under the same holder
grpc_sends_at(call, from, key) :- call_site(call, name, recv), name != "",
    expr_type("client", recv, st), grpc_holder(st, holder),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", call, from),
    key = cat(holder, cat("/", name)).

remote_edge_via(call, from, to, "grpc", key, conf) :- grpc_sends_at(call, from, key),
    grpc_serves(to, key), from != to,
    // the stub's type resolved, so the pairing rests on a type rather than a name alone
    conf = "exact".

// ── gRPC when the generated <S>Grpc holder is NOT in the source tree (#1548) ────────────────
// protoc usually runs at build time, into target/ or build/, so neither the ImplBase nor the stub
// has a java_type row and the rules above never fire. Both ends are still EXTERNAL types
// (resolution/external-types.dl) whose names carry the holder as written: `extends
// OrderServiceGrpc.OrderServiceImplBase` and a field of type `OrderServiceGrpc.OrderServiceBlockingStub`.
// These clauses feed grpc_serves and grpc_sends_at, so the edge rule above is shared.
//
// The shape is checked, not just the suffix: the holder is `<S>Grpc` and the nested type is `<S>`
// plus a server or client suffix, the same <S> on both. An external `RequestStub` or `Foo.BarStub`
// is not a generated stub.
//
// The key is the QUALIFIED holder, read per file. The external node is shared by every file that
// wrote the same name, and one real 2,800-file project imports `MetricsServiceGrpc` from three
// packages (two versions of one API and an unrelated one): keyed on the simple name, each client
// would be paired with all three services. A written package qualifier, then the file's
// single-type import of the holder, then the file's own package when it has no on-demand import,
// which is the order javac resolves the name in. A holder only an on-demand import could supply
// is left unpaired.
grpc_ext_name_demand(x, w) :- external_type(x), strlen(x) > 9,
    w = substr(x, 9, strlen(x) - 9), contains(".", w),
    grpc_generated_suffix(k), strlen(w) > strlen(k),
    substr(w, max(0, strlen(w) - strlen(k)), min(strlen(k), strlen(w))) = k.
grpc_generated_suffix(k) :- cfg_stub_client_suffix(k).
grpc_generated_suffix(k) :- cfg_stub_base_suffix(k, "grpc_service"), !cfg_grpc_holder_suffix(k).
grpc_ext_dot(w, i) :- grpc_ext_name_demand(_, w), i = range(0, strlen(w)), substr(w, i, 1) = ".".
grpc_ext_last_dot(w, l) :- grpc_ext_dot(w, _), l = max i : grpc_ext_dot(w, i).
grpc_ext_prev_dot(w, p) :- grpc_ext_last_dot(w, l), grpc_ext_dot(w, j), j < l,
    p = max i : { grpc_ext_dot(w, i), i < l }.
grpc_ext_has_prev(w) :- grpc_ext_prev_dot(w, _).
// grpc_ext_parts(ExternalType, Qualifier, Holder, Nested): `a.b.FooGrpc.FooStub` -> a.b, FooGrpc, FooStub
grpc_ext_parts(x, pre, h, n) :- grpc_ext_name_demand(x, w), grpc_ext_prev_dot(w, p), grpc_ext_last_dot(w, l),
    pre = substr(w, 0, p), h = substr(w, p + 1, l - p - 1), n = substr(w, l + 1, strlen(w) - l - 1).
grpc_ext_parts(x, "", h, n) :- grpc_ext_name_demand(x, w), !grpc_ext_has_prev(w), grpc_ext_last_dot(w, l),
    h = substr(w, 0, l), n = substr(w, l + 1, strlen(w) - l - 1).
grpc_ext_service(x, s, n) :- grpc_ext_parts(x, _, h, n), cfg_grpc_holder_suffix(hs),
    strlen(h) > strlen(hs), substr(h, max(0, strlen(h) - strlen(hs)), min(strlen(hs), strlen(h))) = hs,
    s = substr(h, 0, max(0, strlen(h) - strlen(hs))).
grpc_ext_server(x) :- grpc_ext_service(x, s, n), cfg_stub_base_suffix(k, "grpc_service"), n = cat(s, k).
grpc_ext_client(x) :- grpc_ext_service(x, s, n), cfg_stub_client_suffix(k), n = cat(s, k).

// the service class that extends the external ImplBase, and the file that wrote the extends
grpc_ext_impl_site(t, x, fp) :-
    java_type_reference(_, "SUPER_TYPE", t, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    type_ref_external(ref, x), grpc_ext_server(x),
    java_type(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, t).
// a call on a receiver typed as an external stub, and the file of the method that makes it
grpc_ext_send_site(call, from, x, fp, name) :- call_site(call, name, recv), name != "",
    expr_type("external", recv, x), grpc_ext_client(x),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", call, from),
    java_method(_, _, _, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, from).

grpc_ext_site(x, fp) :- grpc_ext_impl_site(_, x, fp).
grpc_ext_site(x, fp) :- grpc_ext_send_site(_, _, x, fp, _).
grpc_file_on_demand(fp) :- grpc_ext_site(_, fp), java_import(_, _, _, _, fp, _, "false", "true", _, _, _).
grpc_file_package(fp, pkg) :- grpc_ext_site(_, fp),
    java_type(n, q, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    strlen(q) > strlen(n), pkg = substr(q, 0, max(0, strlen(q) - strlen(n) - 1)).
grpc_file_package(fp, "") :- grpc_ext_site(_, fp),
    java_type(n, n, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", fp, _, _, _, _, _, _).
// grpc_ext_holder_q(ExternalType, File, QualifiedHolder)
grpc_ext_holder_q(x, fp, q) :- grpc_ext_site(x, fp), grpc_ext_parts(x, pre, h, _), pre != "",
    q = cat(pre, cat(".", h)).
grpc_ext_holder_q(x, fp, q) :- grpc_ext_site(x, fp), grpc_ext_parts(x, "", h, _),
    java_import("SINGLE_TYPE", q, _, h, fp, _, "false", _, _, _, _).
grpc_ext_holder_q(x, fp, q) :- grpc_ext_site(x, fp), grpc_ext_parts(x, "", h, _),
    !type_has_explicit_import(h, fp), !grpc_file_on_demand(fp),
    grpc_file_package(fp, pkg), pkg != "", q = cat(pkg, cat(".", h)).
grpc_ext_holder_q(x, fp, h) :- grpc_ext_site(x, fp), grpc_ext_parts(x, "", h, _),
    !type_has_explicit_import(h, fp), !grpc_file_on_demand(fp), grpc_file_package(fp, "").

// An rpc method takes the response StreamObserver: (request, observer) for unary and server
// streaming, (observer) for client and bidi streaming. With the ImplBase absent there is no
// declaration to test an override against, and a helper written beside the rpcs has no observer.
grpc_rpc_shaped(m) :- java_method_parameter(_, _, m, bt, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _), cfg_grpc_observer_type(bt).

grpc_ext_handler(t, x, fp) :- grpc_ext_impl_site(t, x, fp).
grpc_ext_handler(t, x, fp) :- grpc_ext_impl_site(t0, x, fp), type_ancestor(t, t0).
grpc_serves(m, key) :- grpc_ext_handler(t, x, fp), grpc_ext_holder_q(x, fp, q),
    java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "INSTANCE_METHOD", _, _, _, _, m),
    name != "", grpc_rpc_shaped(m), key = cat(q, cat("/", name)).
grpc_sends_at(call, from, key) :- grpc_ext_send_site(call, from, x, fp, name),
    grpc_ext_holder_q(x, fp, q), key = cat(q, cat("/", name)).

// (a) exact — a topic, a queue, or a route both ends spell the same way.
remote_edge_via(call, from, to, tr, d, "exact") :- sends_destination_at(call, from, tr, d),
    serves_destination(to, tr, d), from != to, tr != "http".

// (a2) AMQP through the binding: publisher names exchange+key, listener names a queue.
remote_edge_via(call, from, to, "amqp", dest, "amqp_binding") :- amqp_publish(call, from, exchange, rk),
    amqp_binding(exchange, rk, queue), serves_destination(to, "amqp", queue), from != to,
    dest = cat(exchange, cat("/", rk)).
remote_edge_via(call, from, to, "http", d, "exact") :- sends_destination_at(call, from, "http", d),
    serves_destination(to, "http", d), from != to,
    send_verb(from, sv), method_verb(to, mv), verb_compatible(sv, mv).
// a declarative client's method IS the send, so it has no call site of its own
remote_edge(from, to, "http", d, "exact") :- route_of_method(from, d, "client"),
    serves_destination(to, "http", d), from != to,
    send_verb(from, sv), method_verb(to, mv), verb_compatible(sv, mv).

// (b) route shape — the client concatenated a path where the server declared a
//     template, so the client's string stops at the first variable. This is only
//     applied when the CLIENT wrote no template of its own: matching two templates on
//     their common prefix made /api/x/{id}/reserve and /api/x/{id}/reservation each
//     other's answer, which is a wrong edge rather than a missing one.
remote_edge_via(call, from, to, "http", d, "route_shape") :- sends_destination_at(call, from, "http", d),
    serves_destination(to, "http", s), d != s, from != to,
    send_frag_count(call, 1),
    !contains("{", d), http_path_shaped(d),
    route_prefix(s, d),
    route_segments(d, n), route_segments(s, n),
    !route_contradicted(call, s),
    send_verb(from, sv), method_verb(to, mv), verb_compatible(sv, mv).

// NOTE — a second clause that linked a call whose fragments were ALL contained in a
// route was tried and reverted: `contains(prefix, fragment)` is satisfied by every
// route sharing a prefix, so one call became an edge to four handlers. The prefix
// rule stays anchored on route_prefix equality, and a URL built from several
// fragments that no single route explains stays unlinked and reported.

route_prefix(p, pre) :- http_route(p), !contains("{", p), pre = p.
route_brace(p, i) :- http_route(p), contains("{", p),
    i = range(0, strlen(p)), substr(p, i, 1) = "{".
route_prefix(p, pre) :- http_route(p), contains("{", p),
    m = min i : route_brace(p, i), pre = substr(p, 0, m).
route_segments(p, n) :- http_route(p), n = count : { i = range(0, strlen(p)), substr(p, i, 1) = "/" }.
http_route(p) :- sends_destination(_, "http", p).
http_route(p) :- serves_destination(_, "http", p).

remote_edge(from, to, tr, d, conf) :- remote_edge_via(_, from, to, tr, d, conf).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 5. WHAT COULD NOT BE JOINED — the honest other half
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A send whose destination nothing in this analysis serves. Usually correct — the
// consumer is in another repository — so it is reported rather than treated as a
// defect. It is precisely the list a cross-repository analysis would consume.
// Judged per CALL, not per destination string. A URL built by concatenation yields a
// fragment per piece — "/api/warehouses/", "/us-east", "/capacity" — and reporting each
// of them as an unserved destination, for a call that linked perfectly well, turns the
// honest half into noise.
remote_unserved(m, tr, d) :- sends_destination_at(call, m, tr, d),
    !remote_edge_via(call, _, _, _, _, _).
remote_unserved(m, tr, d) :- route_of_method(m, d, "client"), tr = "http",
    !remote_edge(m, _, "http", d, _).
// NOTE: no separate suppression is needed for an AMQP publish linked through its
// binding — that edge now carries its call like every other, so the one negation above
// covers it. A rule that answered "is this linked?" a second, different way was how a
// destination came to be reported as BOTH linked and unserved.
// A send whose destination cannot be named at all — built at runtime, as in
//     String topic = "orders." + version;  kafka.send(topic, …)
// There is no honest static answer, and SILENCE is the wrong way to say so: a caller
// cannot tell "no destination here" from "a destination I could not read". Declaring it
// is the same contract ambiguous_unknown and config_unresolved already keep.
remote_undetermined(m, tr, "unresolved_destination") :- sends_via(call, tr), tr != "http",
    call_site(call, name, _), cfg_sends_method(name, tr, idx), call_arg(call, idx, arg),
    !arg_destination(arg, _), expr_ultimate_method("client", call, m).
remote_undetermined(m, "http", "unresolved_destination") :- sends_via(call, "http"),
    call_site(call, name, _), cfg_sends_http(name),
    !sends_destination_at(call, _, _, _), expr_ultimate_method("client", call, m).

// A consumer nothing here sends to: the producer is elsewhere.
remote_unsent(m, tr, d) :- serves_destination(m, tr, d), !remote_edge(_, m, tr, d, _).
